## 9. Rules for using the AI builder

- Give it the real route table. Never let it invent stops or times.
- Tell it: "Calculations are done in code. The LLM only explains results."
- Tell it: "Every result shows a label: scheduled / reported / estimated / unknown."
- Tell it: "ETA ranges only from fresh upstream reports; otherwise show scheduled and unknown."
- Test what it builds. Looking finished is not the same as being correct.
- If something doesn't work by the freeze, cut it. Don't half-ship.

## 10. Must-test before demo

- [ ] Zero reports
- [ ] One fresh upstream report gives an ETA range
- [ ] One stale report is marked stale
- [ ] Two conflicting reports show the conflict
- [ ] Report from a stop AFTER the student's stop is ignored for their ETA
- [ ] Fastest vs cheapest changes the ranking
- [ ] Deadline too tight shows a warning
- [ ] AI turned off: results screen still works
- [ ] Works on a phone-sized screen
- [ ] Scenarios match the hand-worked examples

## 11. Demo script (under 5 minutes)

1. Phone-sized view. Show signup with point + stop saved.
2. Show normal scheduled route info.
3. Zero-report state: "Live arrival unknown," not a fake time.
4. Submit an upstream report ("departed stop 3 at 7:40"). The ETA range appears with its source.
5. Trigger **"point already left my stop."** Show "board at a later stop" and alternatives, ranked by fastest/cheapest.
6. Show the AI explanation.
7. Close with the roadmap slide (live tracking, learned ETAs, native app).

Judging: Problem & impact 30% | Scope & buildability 30% | Spec quality 25% | Team readiness 15%.

## 12. Product statement (README and pitch)

> OnRoute helps NED students recover when their usual university shuttle plan fails. It combines verified route information, recent reports from students upstream on the same route, and available travel estimates to compare realistic next steps by time and cost. It shows estimates only when evidence supports them, and says "unknown" when it doesn't.

**Guiding rule: a small reliable flow that works beats many features that don't.**